<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anagarika Dharmapala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anagarika Dharmapala was a Sri Lankan Buddhist reformer and a leader of the Buddhist revival in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. In 1891 he founded the Maha Bodhi Society to restore the Buddhist site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, and he spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> abroad and promoted a Sinhalese Buddhist identity that influenced the later nationalist movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anagarika-dharmapala.jpg" alt="Anagarika Dharmapala" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mahabodhi Society · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dharmapala_color.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dharmapala was born Don David Hewavitarne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> on 17 September 1864, to a family among the richest merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. He attended several Christian and other schools in and around Colombo, including the Colombo Academy. When the Theosophists Helena Blavatsky and Henry Steel Olcott arrived in Ceylon in 1880 and declared themselves Buddhists, he helped Olcott as a translator and took the name Dharmapala, meaning protector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>. As an anagarika, a homeless one midway between monk and layperson, he took the eight precepts for life, which was highly unusual. He eventually broke with the Theosophists over Olcott's universalism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1891 Dharmapala made a pilgrimage to the Mahabodhi Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, where the Buddha attained enlightenment. He was shocked to find it in the hands of a Saivite priest, the Buddha image transformed into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> icon and Buddhists barred from worship, and began an agitation. The Maha Bodhi Society was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> in 1891 and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1892; one of its chief aims was the return of the temple to Buddhist control. Dharmapala brought a lawsuit against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests who had held it for centuries. Success came only in 1949, sixteen years after his death, when the temple's management passed to a committee of equal numbers of Hindus and Buddhists.</p><p>In 1893 he represented Southern Buddhism at the World Parliament of Religions in Chicago, where he read The World's Debt to Buddha and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a>. He travelled and lectured for the next forty years, founded schools and hospitals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and built temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, among them one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>. Mary E. Foster, whom he met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, gave him a donation of over one million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The scholar Gananath Obeyesekere coined the term Protestant Buddhism, often applied to Dharmapala's form of the religion. It stresses the laity and individual experience, and is itself a protest against Christian claims of superiority and missionary work. Dharmapala presented Buddhism as consistent with science, especially evolution, in works such as Evolution from the Standpoint of Buddhism (1926). He also contributed to Sinhalese Buddhist nationalism, with a rhetoric that praised Buddhism and Sinhalese culture and blamed British imperialists and Christians. He was ordained a monk in 1931, took higher ordination in 1933 and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> on 29 April 1933.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anagarika Dharmapala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English education created a new elite, and a Buddhist revival led by <b>Anagarika Dharmapala</b> grew.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-ceylon">British Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.93,79.86&amp;year=1908" aria-label="Colombo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6761 1905 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anagarika Dharmapala"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7211 2175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7211 2175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.93,79.86&amp;year=1908">What was here in 1908? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
